WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:06.560
He has come down through history as America’s
first serial killer, the incarnation of pure

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:07.560
evil.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.700
He was the first of a new breed of American
celebrity – the handsome, debonair and super

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.740
intelligent mass murderer. His legend, fueled
by his own insatiable appetite for exaggeration,

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:21.210
would turn him into a monster, ‘the arch-fiend
of the age’ and the ‘greatest criminal

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:25.680
of this expiring century’. His “Murder
Castle”, situated at the heart of the world’s

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.730
greatest exhibition, was reputedly the scene
of unspeakable horrors.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:34.840
Yet, the truth about Dr. H. H Holmes is far
removed from the sensationalism that surrounds

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:40.250
his name. No one did more to turn the gossip
into legend than Holmes himself, which makes

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:44.010
it all the more difficult to get to the truth
about the Devil in the White City.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:48.450
“I was born with the Devil in me. I could
not help the fact that I was a murderer, more

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:56.420
than a poet can help the inspiration to sing.”

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:02.590
Early Life

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:09.620
The man who would come to call himself Henry
Howard Holmes was born with the far less impressive

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:17.500
name of Herman Webster Mudget on May 16, 1861
in Gilmanton, New Hampshire. By all accounts

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:22.440
his parents were upright and respectable people,
with the child’s upbringing being quite

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:25.850
unremarkable. Later stories that his father
was a violent alcoholic who would smother

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:30.479
his children with chloroform soaked handkerchiefs
to ‘teach them a lesson’ are the result

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:33.020
of a newspaper writer’s overactive imagination.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:38.909
Yet, there is one incident in Herman’s youth
which clearly did have a profound impact on

00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:43.259
his later life... When he was about 13, two
older boys dragged him into the local doctor’s

00:01:43.259 --> 00:01:46.930
office – a place that Herman was terrified
of due to the lurid tales he’d been told

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:51.280
of the body parts that were left lying around.
The bullies brought the terrified boy face

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.920
to face with a human skeleton. But rather
than frightening him, the human bones fascinated

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:01.470
the child. This fascination grew into an adult
desire to study medicine.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:06.570
Holmes had another early fascination – women.
By the age of sixteen he was working odd jobs

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:10.729
when he fell head over heels in love with
a beautiful young woman by the name of Clara

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:15.110
Lovering. Herman met Clara while working on
her father’s farm. Soon after, they were

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:19.489
both at a church social. Clara, though, was
flirting with another boy which made young

00:02:19.489 --> 00:02:24.020
Herman see red. He promptly marched over to
the other boy and threatened to punch his

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:29.680
lights out if he didn’t get lost. This seemed
to impress Clara, with Herman escorting her

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.030
home, arm in arm. The next day he was telling
everyone that they were engaged.

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:38.570
The two – both seventeen years of age – were
married by a Justice of the Peace on July

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:43.560
4th, 1878. For the first six months the marriage
was kept a secret, with the couple living

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.230
apart with their parents. When it was finally
revealed, Herman’s mother wryly commented

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:49.230
. . .

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.870
“She couldn’t have done much worse and
will probably have to support you.”

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:56.670
Clara’s father arranged for Herman to work
in his brother’s grocery store in East Concord.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:03:01.520
Nine months later, Clara gave birth to a son,
who they named Robert. Becoming a father seems

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.520
to have inspired Herman to pursue his interest
in medicine. He quit the store and went home

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.970
to Gilmanton to take up an apprenticeship
under Dr. Wight, the owner of the office in

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:13.760
which he’d been introduced to the skeleton
6 years earlier. Clara and the baby went to

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:20.850
live with her parents…

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:24.400
Medical School

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.159
After a year, the budding medic took up more
formal studies at the medical school in Burlington,

00:03:29.159 --> 00:03:33.739
Vermont. During this time, he conducted himself
as a single man. In fact, he carried on a

00:03:33.739 --> 00:03:38.300
relationship with the daughter of his landlord
that became so passionate that people thought

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:42.450
they were engaged. When Herman’s room-mate,
Fred Ingalls, revealed to the girl’s father

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:47.060
that he was already married, the two-timer
laid a thrashing on Ingalls that left him

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.700
with a black eye and a scratched face.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:55.080
The wife of the owner of the boarding house
once noticed a foul stench emanating from

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:59.880
Herman’s room. On investigating, she was
horrified to find a dead baby under his bed.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:05.209
Upon explaining that he was experimenting
with dissections as part of his ‘homework’,

00:04:05.209 --> 00:04:08.440
Herman was warned to never bring dead bodies
into the house again.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:13.319
In 1882, Herman went to Ann Arbor to study
at the University of Michigan. This time he

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:18.180
took his wife and son with him. But the marriage
was already on shaky ground. Other residents

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:21.829
of the boarding house where they lived later
recalled that the couple often quarreled,

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:26.900
with Clara frequently sporting black eyes.
At some point she decided that she’d had

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.979
enough, returning home to her parents with
baby Robert. The marriage was now effectively

00:04:29.979 --> 00:04:32.360
over though this would never be formalized...

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:37.199
Now unfettered by a family, Herman threw himself
into his studies. He was particularly fascinated

00:04:37.199 --> 00:04:41.860
with the dissection of human bodies, loving
nothing more than to cut his way into flesh

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:46.849
and pull out body organs. Once more he took
to taking home infant corpses to work on during

00:04:46.849 --> 00:04:52.259
the Spring break. His fellow students remembered
that Herman’s fascination with dissection

00:04:52.259 --> 00:04:55.110
was unnatural and unnerving.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:58.419
Whether or not Herman participated in the
ruse of faking someone’s death and using

00:04:58.419 --> 00:05:00.449
a substitute body to defraud an insurance
company during this time is uncertain. In

00:05:00.449 --> 00:05:04.509
his very dubious autobiography, he claims
that he and his medical student pals spoke

00:05:04.509 --> 00:05:08.520
of the idea, but never actually did it. And,
given his penchant for self exaggeration,

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:12.039
it is unlikely he would have been shy about
revealing the fact.

00:05:12.039 --> 00:05:16.729
The one scandal from his college days which
can be substantiated involved, unsurprisingly,

00:05:16.729 --> 00:05:20.819
a woman. Though still married to Clara, he
began courting the woman in whose boarding

00:05:20.819 --> 00:05:25.110
house he resided. He had promised to marry
her, but then the woman found a letter in

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:27.539
his room that was signed by his wife…

00:05:27.539 --> 00:05:30.909
This certainly shocked the woman – but she
should have counted herself lucky… At least

00:05:30.909 --> 00:05:32.689
it wasn’t a dead baby.

00:05:32.689 --> 00:05:36.819
Nevertheless, the woman complained to the
Medical School faculty, citing breach of promise.

00:05:36.819 --> 00:05:40.789
Herman appeared at a hearing and claimed the
woman was lying and that he never promised

00:05:40.789 --> 00:05:44.270
to marry her. The faculty believed him, and
he was acquitted.

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:49.060
A few months later, on being handed his graduation
diploma, Herman approached his professor and

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:50.060
said:

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:55.560
“Doctor, those things that woman said about
me are true.”

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.539
Career

00:05:57.539 --> 00:06:06.650
After graduating, Herman lived with the family
back in New Hampshire during the Summer of

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:10.900
1884. That autumn he began working as both
a physician and a schoolteacher in Mooers

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:15.089
Forks, New York. During this time, he gained
a reputation as a debt defaulter and a womanizer.

00:06:15.089 --> 00:06:19.599
He proposed marriage to two more women there,
with the latter, Minnie Everett, backing out

00:06:19.599 --> 00:06:21.039
with the prophetic reflection . . .

00:06:21.039 --> 00:06:25.199
“There is something lurking in that man’s
character that time will reveal. I do not

00:06:25.199 --> 00:06:28.110
like him. I firmly believe that he would commit
murder.”

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:32.629
Herman also acquired a reputation as a swindler.
He would use any number of excuses to get

00:06:32.629 --> 00:06:35.409
out of paying his rent. In the end he left
Mooers Fork suddenly in the middle of the

00:06:35.409 --> 00:06:40.399
night to escape a mountain of debt. He even
swindled the price of his train ticket out

00:06:40.399 --> 00:06:41.399
of town…

00:06:41.399 --> 00:06:45.889
It was May of 1886, and that swindled train
ticket has taken him to Chicago. It was his

00:06:45.889 --> 00:06:51.310
intention to find work in a drug store, but
he needed a pharmacy license to do so. He

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:55.969
went to Springfield and sat a 3-day examination.
It was subsequently announced in the press

00:06:55.969 --> 00:06:58.919
that “Harry H. Holmes” had passed the
bar.

00:06:58.919 --> 00:07:03.069
This announcement marked the first time that
the Holmes alias was used. Why he chose to

00:07:03.069 --> 00:07:07.740
call himself Harry Howard Holmes is unknown
– contrary to what many think it was not

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:11.809
a nod to Sherlock Holmes; Conan Doyle’s
most famous creation would not appear for

00:07:11.809 --> 00:07:13.069
another year.

00:07:13.069 --> 00:07:16.689
Holmes christened his new name by becoming
a bigamist. On the way to Chicago he had spent

00:07:16.689 --> 00:07:21.159
time in Minneapolis, where he took up with,
and then married, a young woman named Myrta

00:07:21.159 --> 00:07:25.810
Belknap. Myrta was rather plain looking, but
her parents had money and that was enough

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:30.119
for Holmes. Shortly after moving to Chicago,
he used this wealth to purchase a parcel of

00:07:30.119 --> 00:07:36.099
land on what was then 701-703 Sixty-Third
Street in Englewood. He had ownership put

00:07:36.099 --> 00:07:39.339
in his wife’s name, and then into that of
her mother, in order to keep the creditors

00:07:39.339 --> 00:07:40.339
at bay.

00:07:40.339 --> 00:07:44.409
Holmes mythology tells us that the bad doctor
needled his way into the employ of a bedridden

00:07:44.409 --> 00:07:49.459
pharmacist named E.S. Holton, whose young
wife was run off her feet operating the business

00:07:49.459 --> 00:07:54.370
by herself. Holmes was hired to take over
the operation and shortly thereafter the couple

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:59.059
disappeared, with the implication here being
that Holmes murdered them… But this is not

00:07:59.059 --> 00:08:00.180
the reality of the sitation.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:05.099
Instead, Dr. E. S. Holton was actually the
wife. Her husband, rather than being on his

00:08:05.099 --> 00:08:09.919
deathbed, was a robust longshoreman. Dr. Holton
took on Holmes and found him to be an ideal

00:08:09.919 --> 00:08:14.589
asset for the business. When she became pregnant
with her second child in 1887, she decided

00:08:14.589 --> 00:08:18.789
to sell the pharmacy to Holmes. Rather than
disappearing, the Holtons both lived well

00:08:18.789 --> 00:08:22.669
into the 20th Century, remaining in the same
neighborhood that whole time.

00:08:22.669 --> 00:08:27.249
The pharmacy business became a great success,
becoming especially popular with young women

00:08:27.249 --> 00:08:31.990
who came from afar to be served by the charming,
handsome new doctor. Holmes began taking on

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:37.769
assistants, invariably choosing nubile young
beauties who were flocking to Chicago by the

00:08:37.769 --> 00:08:38.769
trainful.

00:08:38.769 --> 00:08:41.870
At the same time, Holmes turned his attention
to the vacant lot he owned on the same street

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:46.209
as the pharmacy. He planned to construct a
two story building, with retail space on the

00:08:46.209 --> 00:08:49.270
first floor and residential apartments on
the second.

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:52.970
The building had some unusual features – there
was a hidden compartment between the first

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:57.019
and second floors, along with a staircase
between floors that could only be reached

00:08:57.019 --> 00:08:59.580
from a trapdoor in the second-story bathroom.

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:03.269
Far more troubling than these design quirks,
however, was the fact Holmes refused to pay

00:09:03.269 --> 00:09:08.300
his bills. When the builders, Aetna Iron and
Steel sued in 1888, Holmes claimed that he

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:12.470
wasn’t liable because the building was actually
owned by his mother-in-law. When the building

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:16.899
company lawyers began to chronicle his involvement
in the project, Holmes alleged that one of

00:09:16.899 --> 00:09:21.480
the steel beams provided was too short, which
negated the entire contract...

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:25.649
Defending himself in court did nothing to
dampen Holmes’ passion for swindling. His

00:09:25.649 --> 00:09:29.420
favorite trick was buying goods on credit
then selling them for cash and not paying

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:34.100
the original bill. In one case he purchased
an especially heavy safe on credit and had

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:38.481
it installed on the first floor of his new
building, having walls built around it. When

00:09:38.481 --> 00:09:41.529
the repossession agents came knocking, Holmes
replied . . .

00:09:41.529 --> 00:09:45.190
Go ahead and take the safe, but I warn you
not to damage the building.

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:48.829
The repossessors struggled for hours, finally
realizing that they couldn’t get it out

00:09:48.829 --> 00:09:52.033
without tearing down a wall. They had to leave
it where it was…

00:09:52.033 --> 00:09:53.033
First Murders

00:09:53.033 --> 00:10:04.709
The 
people who worked for Holmes soon got used

00:10:04.709 --> 00:10:09.040
to his quirky ways. He once invited a worker
to step inside the safe and started yelling

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:13.420
when Holmes closed the door to test if it
was sound proof. His housekeeper would regularly

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:17.589
catch him tip toeing round at night and the
janitor who worked at the pharmacy recalled

00:10:17.589 --> 00:10:21.899
a time when Holmes showed him a collection
of fake beards and other disguises.

00:10:21.899 --> 00:10:26.730
In 1890, Holmes decided to sell the drugstore
business in order to focus on real estate.

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:30.830
Unsurprisingly, the sale turned into another
swindle. After the new owner took possession,

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:35.069
he was shocked to find that much of the floor
stock had not been paid for, with repossession

00:10:35.069 --> 00:10:40.250
agents claiming them back. Still Holmes managed
to talk his way out of trouble. In fact, even

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:44.720
though he no longer owned the drugstore he
still spent a lot of time there. He happened

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:48.970
to be on hand one day when one of the new
investors turned up with important information

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:53.660
to share. The man inexplicably collapsed right
outside the drugstore. Homes was the first

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:58.519
one to his side, pouring a dark liquid down
the man’s throat. Within minutes he was

00:10:58.519 --> 00:10:59.519
dead...

00:10:59.519 --> 00:11:02.430
This was the likely the first person to die
at the hands of Dr. Holmes, killed likely

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:06.570
because he knew too much about the mad medic,
although we’ll never know what that information

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:07.570
really was…

00:11:07.570 --> 00:11:12.839
In July of 1889, Holmes employed a young couple,
Ned and Julia Connor, who worked in the pharmacy

00:11:12.839 --> 00:11:17.740
and lived on the second floor of the recently
completed Englewood building. Within a month,

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:21.820
Holmes and Julia were engaged in a torrid
affair, even as Myrtle lived under the same

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:26.319
roof. This, understandably drove a wedge between
the couple, with Ned filing for divorce and

00:11:26.319 --> 00:11:27.570
quitting Holmes’ employ.

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:32.880
Julia now started to entangle herself in Holmes’
financial web. He listed her as the co-founder

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:38.339
of a number of businesses and took out numerous
debts in her name. Then, on July 4th 1891,

00:11:38.339 --> 00:11:42.769
Julia and her eight-year old daughter Pearl
disappeared off the face of the earth.

00:11:42.769 --> 00:11:46.470
The bodies were never discovered and Holmes
never confessed to murdering them, but it

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:50.350
seems quite likely that they were the next
victims of the deadly doctor.

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:55.180
Around this time, Holmes started a completely
new business, the Warner Glass-bending Company.

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:58.790
Though he actually knew nothing about glass
bending, he convinced those who would listen

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:03.569
that he had invented a unique glass bending
technique. To perfect it he’d built a furnace

00:12:03.569 --> 00:12:07.540
in the basement of his two story building
and made a great show of setting to work,

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:15.670
though no one actually saw him bend any glass.

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:19.990
Serial Killer

00:12:19.990 --> 00:12:23.810
After the establishment of his basement furnace,
the disappearance of young women who came

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:28.220
into Holmes’ circle became more frequent.
First there was Emeline Cigrand, who came

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:33.420
to Chicago looking for work in May, 1892.
Holmes employed her as a typewriter girl and

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:37.470
was soon getting much more for his money...
When the girl disappeared around Christmas

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:40.720
time, Holmes dismissed it with what was to
become a common explanation:

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:43.649
“She’s gone to Europe to get married.”

00:12:43.649 --> 00:12:48.100
By the end of 1892, the whole city of Chicago
was abuzz with the prospect of the approaching

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:53.389
World’s Fair in celebration of the 400th
anniversary of Columbus’ landing on the

00:12:53.389 --> 00:12:57.040
Americas. Never one to pass up an opportunity,
Holmes quickly had a third story put onto

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:01.769
his building to serve as a hotel. However,
he never really intended to use the floor

00:13:01.769 --> 00:13:03.079
as a hotel...

00:13:03.079 --> 00:13:06.649
The amount Holmes could make renting out rooms
was small potatoes compared with what he could

00:13:06.649 --> 00:13:10.920
make by convincing others that he was opening
a hotel. With such a venture in the works,

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:14.879
he could raise money from investors who’d
never see a penny back. He could also buy

00:13:14.879 --> 00:13:19.910
up innumerable goods on credit to sell without
paying for them and, as a final windfall,

00:13:19.910 --> 00:13:23.670
make thousands in insurance money by setting
fire to the building.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:27.149
Despite the popular notion that the Holmes
hotel was flourishing during the World’s

00:13:27.149 --> 00:13:31.879
Fair, luring countless women to their death,
there are no records that he even took in

00:13:31.879 --> 00:13:37.220
a single paying customer. When he set the
structure ablaze on August 13th, 1893, to

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:42.139
claim the insurance, the only people on site
were long term residents. The resulting claim

00:13:42.139 --> 00:13:51.149
would spend years going through the courts,
by which time the claimant would be behind

00:13:51.149 --> 00:13:53.300
bars…

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:55.450
Texas

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:59.759
With mounting lawsuits, Holmes decided that
it was time to quit Chicago. His travels took

00:13:59.759 --> 00:14:04.430
him to Denver, Fort Worth, Texas, St. Louis
and, finally Philadelphia. Along the way he

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:08.810
got married once more, this time to a 23-year
old with a two-thousand dollar inheritance.

00:14:08.810 --> 00:14:13.629
He also fell in with a like minded swindler
by the name of Benjamin Pietzel. Pietzel got

00:14:13.629 --> 00:14:18.269
himself thrown in jail for passing bad checks
and while he was inside, Holmes began paying

00:14:18.269 --> 00:14:23.230
the premiums on his life insurance policy.
When he got out, the two joined forces and

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:27.559
purchased a vacant lot in Fort Worth. Here
they set about building a replica of the complex

00:14:27.559 --> 00:14:30.749
that Holmes had built then burnt to the ground
in Chicago.

00:14:30.749 --> 00:14:34.880
Just like the original building, the Texas
hotel featured a number of strange passages

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:39.100
and twists. But just what Holmes planned to
do with the building is unclear. He didn’t

00:14:39.100 --> 00:14:42.809
hang around long enough to use it. What he
and Pietzel did do was to take out tens of

00:14:42.809 --> 00:14:47.059
thousands of dollars in mortgages on it, and
then disappear without making more than a

00:14:47.059 --> 00:14:48.709
couple of token payments.

00:14:48.709 --> 00:14:52.199
The two moved on to St. Louis where Holmes
bought a drug store for a down payment of

00:14:52.199 --> 00:14:58.059
$50 and promissory notes that were completely
worthless. Pietzel then approached a drugstore

00:14:58.059 --> 00:15:02.589
supplier and convinced them that he was interested
in buying the drugstore from Holmes. If they

00:15:02.589 --> 00:15:06.089
lent him the money, he would use them as his
main supplier. They agreed.

00:15:06.089 --> 00:15:10.089
This swindle came unstuck when the drugstore’s
rep called in the next day, only to find the

00:15:10.089 --> 00:15:15.079
place shuttered up. He got suspicious and
called in the authorities. Holmes was arrested

00:15:15.079 --> 00:15:22.699
and spent three days in jail.

00:15:22.699 --> 00:15:26.069
Downfall

00:15:26.069 --> 00:15:30.350
On his release, Holmes headed for Philadelphia
and the swindle that would prove his undoing.

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:35.149
Here he planned to make good on those insurance
premiums he’d paid for Pietzel a few months

00:15:35.149 --> 00:15:39.300
back. The two men had long been scheming to
defraud the Fidelity Mutual Insurance Company

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:43.759
by faking Pietzel’s death and splitting
the proceeds. In early September, however,

00:15:43.759 --> 00:15:48.029
Peitzel got cold feet and wanted to back out.
An unhappy Holmes agreed to meet up the next

00:15:48.029 --> 00:15:52.589
day to further discuss the issue. When he
turned up at Pietzel’s address, Holmes proceeded

00:15:52.589 --> 00:15:56.720
to get him drunk. Then he knocked him out
with a cholorform handkerchief, increasing

00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:59.699
the dosage until his old friend was dead.

00:15:59.699 --> 00:16:03.340
Holmes then attempted to stage the scene to
make it look like an accident. Then, with

00:16:03.340 --> 00:16:07.939
his 23-year old wife in tow, he jumped on
a train for Indianapolis, intent on securing

00:16:07.939 --> 00:16:13.529
the $10,000 insurance payout. In the end the
payout was made to Pietzel’s widow but Holmes

00:16:13.529 --> 00:16:19.170
convinced her that her husband had died owing
him $7,500 for the Texas building. She handed

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:20.170
the money over.

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:24.860
As an act of graciousness, Holmes agreed to
accompany the three Pietzel children to Indianapolis

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:28.860
where they were to stay with their aunt. Before
he could deliver them, however, he discovered

00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:34.740
that he was a wanted man. Authorities from
a number of states, including Philadelphia,

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:40.980
were keen to talk to him…

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:44.110
Manhunt and Death

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:48.019
Now officially on the run, he moved the children
all over the countryside. At some point, he

00:16:48.019 --> 00:16:52.370
decided that the children would have to die.
The youngest, eight-year old Howard, was poisoned

00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:56.839
with cyanide, while the two girls were likely
gassed to death. The bodies were buried in

00:16:56.839 --> 00:16:57.839
a cellar.

00:16:57.839 --> 00:17:01.160
By now detectives were hot on Holmes’ trail.
They finally caught up with him in Boston

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:05.620
and, of all things, arrested him for horse
theft. A raft of further charges followed

00:17:05.620 --> 00:17:09.829
and in October, 1895, he was convicted of
murdering Benjamin Pietzel.

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:15.539
Holmes subsequently confessed to 27 murders
and was hanged on May 7, 1896. His dying wish

00:17:15.539 --> 00:17:19.679
was for his body to be buried under ten feet
of concrete to prevent grave robbers from

00:17:19.679 --> 00:17:45.769
stealing and dissecting his body – a luxury
he denied many of his victims.

